  "summary": "Strip-woven silk and cotton cloth with intricate geometric patterns",
  "description": "Characterized by bold, colorful geometric designs in narrow woven strips, with each pattern carrying specific cultural significance and historical reference. Traditionally worn by royalty and for special occasions, with colors and patterns encoding social status, achievements, and proverbs.",
  "culturalContext": "Kente — the Asante and Ewe strip-woven silk-and-cotton cloth of Ghana — is Africa's most coded textile: narrow strips (Bonwire looms) sewn into toga-like robes, patterns (adweneasa \"my skill is exhausted\") and colors carrying proverbs, rank and occasion; chiefs wear it with ritual protocol (Okyeame staff, adinkra accents). Gold-and-black royal kente (Kentefuo silk trade history) contrasts Ewe figural weaves; Kente graduation stoles exported it worldwide as Pan-African identity cloth.",
